---
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
const toc = [
  { text: "Automatic snapshots", slug: "automatic" },
  { text: "Restore with a safety checkpoint", slug: "restore" },
  { text: "Named checkpoints", slug: "checkpoints" },
  { text: "The Panic Button", slug: "panic" },
  { text: "Storage & retention", slug: "storage" },
  { text: "Portable history", slug: "portable" },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Game Save Backup, Snapshots & Restore Features | Save Scum"
  heading="A little history. A lot of second chances."
  description="Explore automatic game save snapshots, protected checkpoints, reversible restore, panic saves, and compressed local storage in Save Scum."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Features", href: "/features/" }]}
  toc={toc}
  eyebrow="THE TOOLKIT"
>
  <p>
    Save Scum watches local game save directories and keeps their history
    outside the game’s own save slots. The interface is a library and a
    timeline. Underneath, a Rust engine takes care of capturing, verifying, and
    restoring files.
  </p>
  <h2 id="automatic">Automatic game save backups</h2>
  <p>
    When a configured folder changes, the watcher waits for a quiet period and
    captures the new file state. Changes are batched, ignored files are skipped,
    and unchanged automatic snapshots are suppressed. A second scan checks that
    files did not change during capture; unstable captures are withheld instead
    of recording an incomplete version.
  </p>
  <p>
    Automatic capture requires the desktop app to be running and monitoring
    enabled. A snapshot contains what the game has written to disk, not progress
    still held in memory.{" "}
    <a href="/how-it-works/">Walk through setup and the first snapshot.</a>
  </p>
  <h2 id="restore">A restore with its own way back</h2>
  <p>
    Pick a snapshot, compare changed files, and review the target save folder.
    Close the game before confirming. Save Scum verifies and stages the selected
    files, captures your current state as a protected safety checkpoint, and
    then replaces the live directory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Original files are also kept in a recovery directory beside the save folder.
    Keep those until you have loaded the game and checked your progress. Errors
    and interrupted restores are surfaced for review; no desktop utility can
    promise success through every filesystem or hardware failure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/guides/how-to-restore-overwritten-game-save/">
      Already overwrote a save? Check which recovery options you actually have.
    </a>
  </p>
  <h2 id="checkpoints">Name the moment you want to keep</h2>
  <p>
    Manual checkpoints, notes, labels, and favorites make a long history useful.
    Mark “Before final boss,” “Good ending,” or “Before mod install.” These
    checkpoints are protected from automatic retention cleanup.
  </p>
  <p>
    A manual checkpoint can intentionally preserve unchanged contents. Because
    file data is shared where possible, another named moment does not require
    blindly duplicating every file.
  </p>
  <h2 id="panic">The Panic Button</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ctrl + Shift + F12</strong> triggers a Panic Save for the configured
    target. Use the tray or app if your desktop environment does not support the
    shortcut or another application has claimed it. Global bindings are fixed in
    version 0.1.0.
  </p>
  <p>
    Select the panic target deliberately before a session. The app can also
    choose from monitored games using running-process hints and recent save
    activity. Panic captures are protected from automatic retention. They still
    need readable, stable save files and enough storage.
  </p>
  <h2 id="storage">Shared files. Compressed snapshots.</h2>
  <p>
    Save Scum identifies file contents using BLAKE3 hashes and stores compressed
    data with Zstandard. Snapshots reference the stored files they need. If{" "}
    <code>world.sav</code> is unchanged across ten snapshots, all ten can
    reference the same stored contents. A changed file gets a new stored
    version.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is file-level deduplication, not a promise of tiny binary patches. If a
    game rewrites one huge save file on every save, storage can grow quickly.
    Compression results depend on the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Per-game retention supports count, age, smart, and unlimited policies. The
    newest snapshot, manual and safety checkpoints, panic saves, favorites,
    protected snapshots, and labeled snapshots are kept. Eligible automatic
    snapshots can expire. The storage limit is a soft compressed-data budget;
    leave additional space for staging and original recovery directories.
  </p>
  <h2 id="portable">Take your history with you</h2>
  <p>
    Export a game’s history or a single snapshot as a portable{" "}
    <code>.savescum</code> archive. Imports validate their contents before
    committing metadata and do not overwrite the live save directory. Use a
    separate device for an additional backup against drive failure.
  </p>
  <p>
    The desktop app needs no account, cloud service, or internet connection at
    runtime. Network filesystems and cloud synchronization are not verified
    storage backends.{" "}
    <a href="/docs/">Read the storage and restore documentation</a> before
    moving an existing library.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
